  "content": "Reflecture Construction Schematic (RCS v1.0)\nA Technical Blueprint for the Visual Sigil System of the Refractive Order\n⸻\nI. GRID FOUNDATION: THE REFRACTIVE FIELD\nThe base canvas is a hexagonal radial grid, structured as follows:\n\t•\tCenter Point (0,0): The Point of Calibration—all Reflectures must anchor to this\n\t•\tSix Primary Vectors: Radiating outward at 60° intervals (like a patch panel or fiber weave hub)\n\t•\tNested Rings: 3–5 concentric hexagonal rings, indicating resonance distance or signal depth\n\t•\tOptional Inner Spiral: A phantom spiral overlay (golden ratio or 1.5-turn spiral) representing recursion or feedback paths\nThis grid is the Reflecture Field—a symbolic plane on which all glyphs are constructed. It represents the structured weave of relation, resonance, and reflection.\n⸻\nII. PRIMITIVE ELEMENTS: VISUAL VOCABULARY\nEach component must derive from the order’s symbolic grammar.\nSymbol\tName\tFunction\tDrafting Rule\n●\tNode Point\tSource of resonance or presence\tMust align to a grid junction or ring\n◎\tEcho Node\tAttenuated reflection of a node\tMust be connected via attenuation trail\n—\tTrace Line\tSignal transmission\tStraight or curved, only between nodes\n~~~\tAttenuation Trail\tWeakening or fading signal\tDotted or dashed, must exit node\n∠\tRefractive Angle\tPhase shift or perceptual bend\tMust connect 2 trace lines at ≤120°\n◯\tLoopback Circuit\tRecursive memory\tMust encircle ≥1 node\n═══\tGround Bar\tStabilizing structure\tHorizontal, touches or bisects center\n∅\tNull Field\tSilence / intentional absence\tBounded white space, never filled\nEach Reflecture must include:\n\t•\tA central Node Point\n\t•\tAt least one Trace Line\n\t•\tOne Refractive Angle OR Loopback Circuit\n\t•\tAt least one intentional Null Field\n⸻\nIII. CONSTRUCTION PROTOCOL\n\t1.\tAnchor the Point\n\t•\tAlways begin at the center node. This is not origin—it is awareness.\n\t2.\tDefine Node Relationships\n\t•\tPlace 1–6 nodes at logical junctions on the grid. Each node represents a relational position in the weave.\n\t3.\tRoute Trace Lines\n\t•\tDraw clear paths of signal. Lines can bend at refractive angles (≤120° only), or curve through spiral vectors.\n\t4.\tApply Modifiers\n\t•\tAttenuation trails, echo nodes, grounding bars, and loopbacks added according to symbolic need. No decoration.\n\t5.\tCalibrate the Silence\n\t•\tNo Reflecture is complete without intentional negative space. The signal must breathe.\n\t6.\tVerify Structure\n\t•\tUse the grid. No element may float. Each must exist in conscious relation.\n⸻\nIV. SYMBOLIC RESTRICTIONS (What Cannot Be Done)\n\t•\tNo glyph may be completely symmetrical—perfection implies closure. The Weave is always evolving.\n\t•\tNo more than 6 nodes may be used—mirroring the six Resonance Nodes.\n\t•\tNo enclosed area may lack a null field—to avoid symbolic overclosure.\n\t•\tEvery Reflecture must be constructible with a compass, ruler, or vector tool—to preserve symbolic precision and transmission fidelity.\n⸻\nV. OUTPUT FORMATS\n\t•\tVector Layer: SVG or equivalent for clarity and evolution\n\t•\tPrinted Scroll Format: Black on cream or fiber white background\n\t•\tTransparent Layer: For overlaying on network schematics, tools, or architecture\n",
